text:
Maya P.: heads-up — our Next middleware started throwing during canary: `ReferenceError: setInterval is not defined` when attempting token refresh in-flight
Ravi N.: I can repro on Next 13.4 edge target. it only happens when the SDK's auth helper tries to schedule retries from middleware.
Jordan T.: yep, edge runtimes intentionally omit timer APIs. any code that assumes Node globals will blow up. also saw `Error: require is not defined` in the bundled output when using some CJS entrypoints.
Maya P.: we need to mint a short-lived token before proxying. can't move everything out of middleware. what do folks recommend?
Samira V.: options we tested:
- synchronous check: read token exp from cookie/jwt and synchronously call a lightweight refresh endpoint if near-expiry (no timers needed)
- proxy endpoint: let middleware quickly POST to a small serverless route that can run node timers if needed (but adds one hop)
- careful polyfill: add tiny setTimeout polyfill that only uses microtasks — okay for immediate defers but destroys backoff semantics

Ravi N.: microtask polyfills made our retry logic spin (no backoff) and hit the auth server too hard during bursts. not acceptable.
Maya P.: can you show the synchronous approach example?
Samira V.: paste:

```
// middleware.ts (edge)
import { NextResponse, type NextRequest } from 'next/server'
import { createClient } from '@redwood/inference-js'

export default async function middleware(req: NextRequest) {
  const cookies = req.cookies.get('session')?.value || '';
  // quick parse of JWT exp claim (no network, pure sync)
  const exp = (() => {
    try {
      const payload = JSON.parse(atob(cookies.split('.')[1] || ''));
      return payload?.exp || 0;
    } catch { return 0 }
  })();

  const now = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
  if (exp - now < 30) {
    // token about to expire, call a tiny refresh route that returns a short lived token
    const res = await fetch(new URL('/api/refresh', req.url), { method: 'POST', body: cookies });
    if (!res.ok) return NextResponse.rewrite(new URL('/login', req.url));
    const json = await res.json();
    const token = json.access;
    const r = NextResponse.next();
    r.headers.set('authorization', `Bearer ${token}`);
    return r;
  }

  return NextResponse.next();
}
```

This avoids any scheduled timers inside middleware.
Jordan T.: couple extra notes:
- ensure you import the SDK as ESM in edge code: `import { createClient } from '@redwood/inference-js'` — avoid CJS require paths, they break the edge bundler.
- SDK should export a `runtime: 'edge'` hint so bundlers/tree-shakers can skip node-only code.
- TypeScript: annotate middleware arg as `NextRequest` to satisfy overloads.

Maya P.: the SDK team — do we already have an edge-safe mode?
SDK CI: auto-build status: failing tests on edge matrix due to timers in auth code.
Samira V.: I opened a small SDK change that gates backoff/retry on presence of timers. when runtime:'edge' is provided, the SDK falls back to immediate retry attempts (no setTimeout) and surfaces a warning. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk/pull/1267
Ravi N.: will that change the public API? we need to be explicit in docs so folks don't assume identical semantics on edge.
Samira V.: no API surface change, just a runtime hint option: `createClient({ apiKey, runtime: 'edge' })`. also added `edge-middleware.md` example and a short note about TTL-check pattern.
Maya P.: perfect. can we get a patch cut this week? customers waiting.
